WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.169
-Our first guest tonight is
a Tony and Emmy winning actor,

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.371
you know from "Boardwalk
Empire" and "Westworld,"

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.708
as well as the "James Bond"
and "Hunger Games" franchises.

00:00:08.774 --> 00:00:11.210
He just received his first
Academy Award nomination

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:13.345
for Best Actor
in "American Fiction,"

00:00:13.412 --> 00:00:14.581
which is in theaters now.

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.815
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:18.183
-Here you go.
-All right.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.919
Yeah. Wait a minute.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.854
Why -- Why are these books here?

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.222
-I'm not sure.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:27.627
I would imagine that
this author, Ellison, is black.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.662
-That's me. Ellison.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:33.065
Yeah. He is me.

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:34.667
And he and I are black.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:35.868
-Oh, bingo.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.436
-No. Bingo, Ned.

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:40.706
These books have nothing to do
with African American studies.

00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:42.141
They're just literature.

00:00:42.207 --> 00:00:45.144
The -- The blackest thing
about this one is the ink.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.312
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.213
our friend Jeffrey Wright,
everybody.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.316
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:55.421 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.488 --> 00:01:00.526 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:02.495
Welcome back.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:03.663
-Thank you.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.667
-Oscar Academy Award nominee.
-Yeah.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.034
That happened.
-That happened.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:10.169
-Yeah.
-I feel like

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.471
this is a real
compliment to you.

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:13.206
And I don't know
if you've noticed it,

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.607
but I think when this happened,

00:01:14.674 --> 00:01:16.509
a lot of people
were saying things like,

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.077
"Oh, I can't believe
it hasn't happened before."

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:19.578
Now, of course,
you were well aware

00:01:19.645 --> 00:01:21.680
that it never happened before,
but it must have been nice

00:01:21.747 --> 00:01:23.249
that you'd done work
over your career,

00:01:23.315 --> 00:01:25.284
that people were surprised
in the moment to be like,

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:27.052
"Oh, I just assumed Jeffrey
would have had one by now."

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:30.156
-Yeah, I suppose
that that's true.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.792
And what's wonderful
about this, too,

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:35.928
is that it's acknowledgment
from our peers that,

00:01:35.994 --> 00:01:38.264
you know, "Job well done,
we appreciate your work."

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.501
So I've had the opportunity over
the course of the last weeks

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.904
to run into people,
actors that I admire

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.872
who said, you know,
"I really like your work."

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.508
And it's been a lot,

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.010
but it's been really touching.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.445
-That's really nice.
-It's been really cool, yeah.

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:55.080
I'm grateful.
-What were your expectations

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:56.815
on the morning
when the nominations came out,

00:01:56.882 --> 00:01:59.319
and how were you receiving
that information?

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:03.422
-Well, I didn't watch
the announcement ceremony.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:08.995
I didn't want any sudden
unforeseen damage to screens.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:12.565
You know, in case the news
was not to my liking.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.267
You know,
I wanted to save those --

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:16.869
save those repair costs.
-Yeah.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:19.938
-So I just kind of
wandered around my house

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:21.073
was in my office a bit.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.809
I had the phone
on a coffee table there,

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:27.513
and I just waited
and it started to light up

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.482
and it seemed like good news.

00:02:29.548 --> 00:02:33.385
So, yeah,
but I ignored the thing.

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:35.721
And then I looked up
and I saw a picture of my mom.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:39.425
My mom passed a few years ago,
just about a year or so

00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:41.394
before I got the script,
and we had a moment

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:42.628
and that was nice.
-That's wonderful.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:43.996
-First person I talked to.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.265
-And you had -- Your daughter
was in the house?

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:47.800
-My daughter was not
in the house.

00:02:47.867 --> 00:02:49.268
-Okay.
-So my daughter had been on

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:52.104
leave from school for Christmas.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.374
So it was her last night
in New York,

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:59.078
so she decided to go to one
of her best friend's houses

00:02:59.144 --> 00:03:01.948
the night, uh --
the night before.

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:05.617
And, you know, she was up late,
so she was asleep.

00:03:05.684 --> 00:03:08.053
And then she came home,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.689
and I'm doing interviews,

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.224
you know,
doing the Zoom interviews.

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.826
People want to know, you know,
your reaction and all that.

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.194
And she's downstairs,

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:17.362
and I hear her like,
you know, having breakfast.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:21.000
I'm like, "Hey, uh, what?
You're gonna come up?"

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:25.004
And she's like,
you know, "I'm having eggs.

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:27.239
As soon as I finish
these eggs, I'll be up."

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:29.208
You know, I'm like,
"Well, come on up."

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:32.945
You know, she eventually
comes up and, uh -- and I said,

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:35.647
"You know why I'm doing
these interviews, yeah?"

00:03:35.714 --> 00:03:37.016
And she freaked out.
She realized.

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.784
-Oh, that's good.
So she finally came to it?

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:39.985
Yeah.
-Yeah, she finally came around.

00:03:40.052 --> 00:03:41.520
-She was the last one to know.

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:42.855
-Yeah, she was.

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:44.423
-And then you mentioned
she was home from school.

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.726
You then had to spend the day
of your nomination news,

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:48.995
you drove her back to college.

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:50.963
-That was my celebration. Yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.398
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:52.464 --> 00:03:54.700
Let's hop in the car
and drive up to New England.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:55.768
Yeah.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:57.370
-Just a chauffeur.
-But she --

00:03:57.437 --> 00:03:59.939
I mean, that's, you know,
my family, like my -- my --

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:02.608
I had the good fortune
of being raised by two women.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.511
My mother
and her eldest sister,

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.847
my aunt Naomi,
who after my mom passed,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:10.082
came to live with us
for a couple of years.

00:04:10.148 --> 00:04:13.219
And then I was able to
build a house down in Virginia

00:04:13.286 --> 00:04:14.220
where they're from.
-Oh, wonderful.

00:04:14.286 --> 00:04:15.588
-So she's moved back down there.

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:17.456
She's 94 years old.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:20.359
-Her brain is sharper
than mine, believe me.

00:04:20.425 --> 00:04:22.695
Which is not saying much,
but, um,

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.097
so she -- I don't hear from her.

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.299
I call her up, I say, uh --

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.936
because she has
trouble seeing now,

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:33.105
eyes are faded a bit, so she has
difficulty dialing the phone,

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.807
but I call her up
and I say, uh,

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:38.711
"Hey, uh, Nay, did you hear
any news this morning?"

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:42.248
She goes, uh, "Yeah, yeah,
I heard that.

00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:44.116
Yeah, yeah."
-[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:46.319
-"Yeah, that's great.

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.287
But you should have been
nominated years ago."

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.089
-There you go.
-So, you know.

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:52.291
-There you go,
you got to honor the vote here.

00:04:52.358 --> 00:04:53.492
-Yeah.
-[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.294
-But that that, you know,

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.263
that's family, you know,
that's my family.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.031
"You should have
done it years ago,"

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:01.700
and, you know,
between that and, uh, "Sorry,

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:03.603
I'm eating my eggs,"
you know?

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:05.971
-I just like that your whole
morning with, uh,

00:05:06.038 --> 00:05:07.506
after you got nominated
was walking around

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.575
to family members being like,
"So, did you hear anything?"

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:11.310
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:11.376 --> 00:05:13.413
And when my aunt,
I'm not sure of what she said

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.382
was like an indictment of me
or of the system.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.651
-Yeah, exactly.
-System's wrong, man.

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:20.319
-Right. That's the way I saw it.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.554
But it'd be funnier
if she's like,

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.455
"Why have you been doing
work like this earlier?"

00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:26.292
-There's dual meaning there.
There is definitely.

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:28.094
-I want to talk about the work,

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:29.495
I want to talk
about the great film.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:31.330
We'll be right back with more

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:32.765
from Jeffrey Wright after this.

